have you ever run a workload that uses  virtual machines in google cloud and  while you're enjoying a cup of tea  during quick break your alerts are  firing the service is having issues and  users are really unhappy  you rush back to your computer and log  into the cloud console the vms are still  up and system resources are seemingly  normal what can you do to troubleshoot  from here  well you might want to look at a few  things whether your app processes are  running on the vms how much resources  they're consuming and whether there are  any errors in the logs  but as you may remember from our  previous episode  you need to have the ops agent installed  running and collecting and sending data  to get this kind of information from  your vms  if you ever have issues getting  installed running or getting data you've  come to the right place my name is yuri  i'm an sre at google cloud and today i'm  going to show you how to troubleshoot  the alps agent to make sure you always  have the vm observability data you need  and get back to that tee before it gets  cold  let's start at the beginning with the  installation there are two primary  reasons why you might have trouble  getting the agent installed  the first reason is that you're trying  to install it on an unsupported  operating system you'll know that's the  case if you see an error message like  this where the installer isn't able to  download the right package for your os  the second reason is a configuration  conflict specifically with the old  monitoring or logging agents in this  case you'll see an error message like  this one  you can't have both the alps agent and  the old agents installed at the same  time so you'll have to uninstall the old  agents first  once the agent is installed you can  confirm that it's running by using the  system cdl status command like this and  looking for the confirmation messages  that the agent components are started  and running  if the agent is not running you may need  to start it if it has issues starting  you may have a conflict or an invalid  configuration let's have a look at how  to start the agent and sort out startup  issues  if the agent isn't running for any  reason you'll see a message like this  saying that the service isn't running  when you check its status  use the start command to try to get it  running again  you might get a message like this saying  that either the cloud ops agent itself  or one of its modules failed to start  the former usually means you have an  invalid configuration  have a look at the documentation on how  to configure the agent to sort this out  we'll of course link to it in the  episode notes  if a module can't start it's likely  because it can't send logs or metrics to  the api often this is caused by invalid  credentials such as a disabled service  account or an expired key networking  issues and proxy issues such as an  invalid proxy configuration can also  cause this  once you verify that the agent is  installed and running you should be  getting its metrics and logs ingested if  that's not the case you'll need to  figure out why  generally you'll be investigating three  types of issues the first has to do with  the agent itself the second is with the  sources of data you're trying to ingest  and the third is with sending collected  data to the respective api let's have a  look at each  if the issue you're seeing has to do  with logs check to see if the logging  module is running like this if the  output is anything other than successful  you may need to restart the agent or  validate its configuration  if the logging module is running the  next thing to check is whether it's able  to read the logs one way to check is to  use the local metric standpoint like  this  if everything looks good so far but  you're still not seeing the logs you  expect check to see if the agent is  getting any errors from the logging api  you can do that in the logging module  log like this you might see permission  errors if the api isn't enabled in the  project the service account has been  disabled or doesn't have the right  permissions the service account key  expired or if the agent is using the  vm's default service account  insufficient scopes on the vm itself you  might also be out of quota for the api  you'll need to resolve these to resume  log ingestion  we'll use a similar approach to  troubleshoot the metrics module let's  have a look  you can check the status of the metrics  module by looking at module logs in  syslog if there aren't any logs the  service may not be running properly  otherwise you can check the logs  themselves to see if there are any  issues noted there  this log is also where you would check  for any api errors such as permission  denied or quota issues  at this point you should have everything  you need to sort out agent issues and  never be in the dark about your vms  running on google cloud  thanks so much for watching today don't  forget to like and subscribe to never  miss out on more engineering for  reliability with google cloud see you  soon  [Music]
 

